  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to find an improved upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap integral problem by evolving a population of binary solutions using a genetic programming-like approach with modifications. The current program's local optimization might get stuck in suboptimal regions, so we introduce a population-based search to explore more diverse solutions. The primary approach involves:\n- Maintaining a population of candidate solutions represented as binary arrays (0/1)\n- Evolving the population through crossover and mutation operations designed to preserve the sum constraint (exactly half ones)\n- Using a tournament selection mechanism to favor better candidates\n- Implementing a crossover operation that preserves half the ones in each offspring\n- Using mass transfer mutation to explore new configurations\n- Employing a Pareto-style refinement that simultaneously applies gradient descent and stochastic perturbation\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration of the solution space by local optimization alone. This approach targets regions not easily reachable through gradient-based methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent candidate solutions as binary arrays h of length n_points (initially 512) where exactly n_points/2 values are 1.0 (uses sum_constraint).\n\nThe evaluation objective (C5) is computed as:\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1.0-h, mode=\"full\")\n    C5 = np.max(corr) * (2.0/n_points)\n\nWe maintain two distributions of candidate solutions:\n- A \"permanent\" population with top performers\n- A \"temporal\" population with recent finds\n\nEach solution must satisfy:\n    - Values in [0,1]\n    - Sum exactly n_points/2\n\nWe define the following operations:\n\nCrossover:\n    For two parent solutions h1 and h2, select a random cutoff index. Then create two children:\n        child1 = h1[:cutoff] concatenated with h2[cutoff:]\n        child2 = h2[:cutoff] concatenated with h1[cutoff:]\n    Each child is then adjusted to ensure exactly n_points/2 ones.\n\nMutation:\n    For a scalar \u03b1, randomly select positions where h[i] is 0 to increment, then select positions where h[i] is 1 to decrement by (1-\u03b1), ensuring constraints remain valid.\n\nRefinement:\n    Use a combined gradient descent and stochastic adjustment where:\n        - Gradient descent uses the maximal correlation shift direction\n        - Stochastic adjustment adds small perturbations to escape local minima\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n    - If `initial_h_values` is available, use it as the primary solution\n    - Initialize two population dictionaries: permanent and temporary\n    - Set target_sum = n_points/2\n\n2. Population generation:\n    - `generate_candidate()`: Create a valid candidate by generating a probability vector, enforcing sum constraint, then discretizing to binary\n\n3. Evaluation:\n    - Compute correlation on CPU side to avoid FFT overhead\n    - Track best solution and update distributions\n\n4. Evolution:\n    - Tournament selection from permanent population\n    - Apply crossover to selected parents\n    - Apply mutation to new candidates\n    - Update permanent and temporary populations by tournament selection and crowding avoidance\n\n5. Refinement phase:\n    - Use an array of alphas with decreasing magnitude\n    - At each step, combine gradient descent with random perturbation\n    - Monitor and update best solution\n    - After refinement, produce new candidates from best solutions\n\n6. Termination:\n    - When time budget expires, return best solution found\n    - Ensure final solution satisfies both constraints exactly\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Global variables for tracking: CURRENT_TARGET (sum constraint), temp_alpha_schedule, input_alpha_schedule, run_start_time\n- Crossover depends on random cutoff index with L1/L2 crossover probabilities\n- Mutation has adjustable noise scale (sqrt_alpha)\n- Refinement schedule adapts based on remaining time\n- Population size stays fixed at 100\n- Time checks occur every processing step\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Time budget: 1100 seconds per run\n- Most time is spent on correlation calculation (O(n^2))\n- Main loop has time boundary checks\n- Gradient calculation is O(n^2) per step\n- Always returns the best feasible solution\n\n6. Validation and contract\n- Feasibility checks: 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)==n_points//2\n- Objective calculation uses discrete correlation with proper scaling\n- Best solution returned via compute_c5\n- Evaluation respects the original Erd\u0151s problem formulation\n\nRepresentation and dimensions:\n- Inputs: n_points, target_sum, seed (optional)\n- All arrays: [samples] shape\n- Operations maintain the problem constraints exactly\n\nImplementation details:\n- Mutated/mixed distributions tracked by time-stamped dictionaries\n- Best solution updated whenever better found\n- The crossover and mutation methods preserve binary representations\n- Structured schedule divides the budget between main evolution and refinement\n\nThis plan provides a structured, efficient search for improved Erd\u0151s constants within the given constraints.",
